"""Interaction Design module."""
from _lib import *

write("split-buttons",
 "A split button pairs a labelled primary action with a small dropdown arrow. Users treat it as one button, read the chevron as decoration, and never find what's behind it.",
 [("What users actually do",
   "In Calabro's recent tests, **about 80% of participants clicked only the labelled side**, never realising there was more under the arrow.\n\nUsers who *intended* to open the menu often triggered the primary action instead — an accidental purchase, or the wrong state.\n\nThe tiny chevron reads as decoration.",
   cmp("What the team assumes", ["Two clear actions in one control", "The arrow invites exploration", "Saves horizontal space"],
       "What testing shows", ["One button, one action", "The chevron looks decorative", "Secondary options never found"]),
   q("Roughly what share of participants clicked only the labelled side in his tests?",
     ["About 80%", "About 20%", "About half", "Nearly everyone, apart from screen reader users"],
     "They never realised there was more functionality under the arrow.")),
  ("Why stakeholders keep asking for them",
   "Split buttons let a team avoid a hard decision about priority. “We can put every possible action in one button? Cool.”\n\nIt feels like a compromise that saves space. **Saving space is not the same as reducing cognitive load.**\n\nThree e-commerce clients asked Calabro to validate theirs. All three pushed back — and during a demo, one of them mis-clicked the arrow himself.", None,
   q("What hard question does a split button let a team avoid?",
     ["Which action is primary", "Which users are the target", "Which platform to design for first", "Whether the feature should exist at all"],
     "That is exactly the question the pattern is designed to dodge.")),
  ("What to do instead",
   "If the secondary actions matter, expose them as **separate buttons** or a clear **overflow menu** — not hidden behind an ambiguous chevron attached to a primary action.\n\nAesthetic adjustments don't help: one client tried multiple rounds of visual refinement to emphasise the split, and end-user behaviour didn't change at all.", None,
   q("A client emphasises the visual split between the two halves. What happens to behaviour?",
     ["Nothing changes", "Discovery roughly doubles", "Mis-clicks fall but discovery stays low", "Screen reader users benefit, others don't"],
     "Multiple rounds of visual refinement didn't alter end-user behaviour."))],
 [q("What is a split button?",
    ["A labelled action plus a small dropdown arrow", "Two buttons separated by a divider line", "A button that changes label based on context", "A toggle that reveals a second action on hover"],
    "The two halves do different things."),
  q("What does the chevron communicate to most users?",
    ["Nothing — it reads as decoration", "That the button has a secondary menu", "That the action is irreversible", "That more options load after clicking"],
    "Which is why hidden options stay hidden."),
  q("Why is a split button especially risky in a destructive flow?",
    ["Users trigger the primary action by mistake", "Destructive actions need confirmation dialogs", "The arrow is too small for touch targets", "Screen readers announce the label twice"],
    "Intending the menu and hitting the action is a common failure in testing."),
  q("What does Calabro recommend in place of a split button?",
    ["Separate buttons or a clear overflow menu", "A dropdown containing every action", "A context menu on right-click", "A single button plus a help tooltip"],
    "Force the priority decision instead of hiding it."),
  q("Why doesn't “our users are on the site all the time” hold up?",
    ["Even that client mis-clicked during the demo", "Frequent users read chevrons more carefully", "Repeat visitors avoid dropdown menus", "Site frequency doesn't affect motor accuracy"],
    "Familiarity didn't rescue the pattern, even for its defender.")],
 summary="About 80% never open the menu; the chevron reads as decoration. Use two buttons or an overflow menu.")

write("navigation",
 "People search for information the way animals forage for food. They follow a trail of clues about what lies ahead — **information scent**. Navigation fails when labels stop giving off scent.",
 [("Information foraging",
   "Pirolli and Card's information foraging theory: users follow clues that suggest what content or action lies ahead.\n\nNone of this is new. Information scent is a framing for principles you already know — starting with Nielsen's *match between system and the real world*, or as Calabro puts it: **talk like a human, not a product manager**.", None,
   q("What is information scent?",
     ["The clues that suggest what lies behind a link", "The visual weight given to key navigation items", "The time it takes users to find a page", "A ranking of pages by how often they are visited"],
     "Users predict where their goal lives, then follow the trail.")),
  ("Where scent goes to die",
   "Bad scent is usually the result of compromise, not sabotage: a label rewritten for a marketing push, a category renamed after the org chart.\n\n• **Vague labels** — “Explore”, “Solutions”, “Resources”, “Experience”\n• **Icons without text** — a gear or lightning bolt is a guessing game\n• **Overlapping categories** — “Help Center”, “Support Articles” and “FAQs” side by side\n• **Branded language** that doesn't match user vocabulary\n• **Org-chart IA**",
   cmp("Weak scent", ["Explore", "Solutions", "Resources"],
       "Strong scent", ["Track my orders", "Manage my account", "Get help"]),
   q("A site has “Help Center”, “Support Articles” and “FAQs” in the same menu. What's the problem?",
     ["Overlapping categories give no clear trail", "Three links is too many for a help section", "Support content should live behind login", "The labels are too long for a top nav"],
     "Users can't predict which one holds their answer.")),
  ("How to spot it and fix it",
   "**Spot it:** tree tests and card sorts, 5-second tests, click tracking, and quotes from usability tests like “I wouldn't have clicked that.”\n\n**Fix it:** plain language that matches what users search for; key tasks visible from the main entry points; combine or rename categories that are too similar; add short explanatory text; and if you use icons, always pair them with labels.", None,
   q("Which method tells you whether users can predict what a section contains?",
     ["A tree test", "A/B testing two layouts", "A satisfaction survey", "A heuristic review by two experts"],
     "Tree tests and card sorts check the IA against user expectations."))],
 [q("Whose theory is information foraging?",
    ["Pirolli and Card's", "Nielsen and Norman's", "Pirolli and Nielsen's", "Card and Moran's"],
    "They studied how people search for information the way animals forage."),
  q("Why do labels usually lose their scent?",
    ["Through compromise, not bad intent", "Because designers prefer short words", "Because translations lose meaning", "Because analytics reward clever names"],
    "A marketing rewrite here, an org-chart rename there."),
  q("What is wrong with “Explore” as a top-level label?",
    ["It gives no clue what's behind it", "It is too informal for enterprise products", "It duplicates the search function", "It performs poorly on small screens"],
    "Vague labels are the first category of scent failure."),
  q("What does a 5-second test tell you here?",
    ["Whether users can say what a section is about", "How long the page takes to load", "Which link gets the most clicks", "Whether the layout works on mobile"],
    "It checks whether the label and context communicate quickly."),
  q("Why is “scent” a useful word with stakeholders?",
    ["It explains that users are following clues, not failing", "It sounds more scientific than usability", "It avoids blaming the marketing team", "It maps directly to analytics metrics"],
    "It gives non-UX colleagues language for what is going wrong.")],
 summary="Users follow clues. Vague labels, icons without text and org-chart IA kill the trail.")

write("carousels",
 "Baymard published “10 UX Requirements for User-Friendly Carousel Design”. Its first hundred words concede carousels aren't great; the next 2,000 explain how to do them right. That's the problem.",
 [("A best-practice guide for a bad pattern",
   "Calabro's objection isn't that the advice is wrong. It's that a how-to guide **gives teams cover** to keep using a pattern the evidence says to avoid.\n\nThe structure of the article is: “carousels don't work” … “but here's how to make one anyway.”",
   cmp("Softened", ["“Carousels don't work…”", "“…but here's how to do it anyway”", "Reads as permission to ship it"],
       "Honest", ["Say plainly that it fails", "If it's harm reduction, say so", "Point at the alternative first"]),
   q("What is Calabro's main objection to the Baymard article?",
     ["It legitimises a pattern the evidence rejects", "Its ten requirements are technically wrong", "It was published without original research", "It ignores accessibility entirely"],
     "A guide to doing it right reads as permission to do it.")),
  ("He grants the counter-arguments",
   "• Everyone uses them anyway, so help make the least-bad version\n• It's a stopgap for teams still phasing them out\n• Stakeholders insist, and this gives UX folks something concrete\n• Reduced harm beats no guidance\n\nHe doesn't think any of these is completely wrong. **His point: if harm reduction is the rationale, say so out loud.**", None,
   q("A team genuinely can't remove its carousel this quarter. What does Calabro ask of guidance written for them?",
     ["Say plainly that it's harm reduction", "Withhold the guidance entirely", "Add a disclaimer about sample sizes", "Require sign-off from a researcher"],
     "He accepts the stopgap; he objects to presenting it as best practice.")),
  ("The bigger pattern",
   "This isn't really about carousels. It's about a trend of **watering down strong evidence** so it feels less confrontational.\n\nWe don't want to alienate teams. We want to be helpful. So we offer guidance that makes a bad pattern slightly less bad — and the pattern survives another five years.", None,
   q("What industry habit does the article criticise?",
     ["Softening strong evidence to stay agreeable", "Publishing research without peer review", "Relying on competitor analysis", "Testing with too few participants"],
     "Diplomatic dishonesty, in the article's phrase."))],
 [q("What does the article say about the first hundred words of the Baymard post?",
    ["They already concede carousels aren't great", "They overstate the evidence against carousels", "They promise data that never appears", "They define the pattern incorrectly"],
    "Then 2,000 words follow on how to do one anyway."),
  q("Which counter-argument does Calabro accept as reasonable?",
    ["Stopgap guidance for teams phasing carousels out", "Carousels work well when auto-rotation is off", "Carousel performance depends on the industry", "Users have learned to expect carousels by now"],
    "He grants several practical reasons; he wants them stated."),
  q("What does “diplomatic dishonesty” refer to?",
    ["Phrasing that softens what the evidence says", "Disagreeing privately but agreeing in public", "Citing research you haven't read", "Avoiding criticism of named companies"],
    "Such as “don't perform adequately” for “consistently underperform”."),
  q("Why does a how-to guide matter more than an opinion piece here?",
    ["Teams cite it as permission to ship the pattern", "Guides reach a larger audience", "Opinion pieces are rarely read by stakeholders", "Guides are harder to correct later"],
    "The format itself grants legitimacy."),
  q("What is the article ultimately about?",
    ["How the UX industry communicates evidence", "The specific usability of carousels", "Baymard's research methodology", "E-commerce homepage conversion rates"],
    "Carousels are the example, not the subject.")],
 summary="A how-to guide for a pattern the evidence rejects gives teams cover — say it's harm reduction, or say don't.")

write("nav-guide",
 "A spec-ready rule set for primary navigation, refined over ten years and written to hand to engineering. Most navigation failures come from hover-driven behaviour and split rows.",
 [("Two item types, no hybrids",
   "**Destination items** are links with no indicator.\n**Menu items** are buttons with an indicator and an expanded/collapsed state.\n\n**No split rows.** A row navigates or opens a menu, never both. If the parent has its own page, put an “Overview” link inside the menu.",
   cmp("Breaks navigation", ["Hover opens or closes menus", "Item both navigates and expands", "A tiny chevron is the only target"],
       "Calabro's rules", ["Only click, tap, Enter or Space", "Link OR button; parent gets “Overview”", "Targets ≥ 24×24 px, spaced"]),
   q("A top-level item both links to a section page and opens a submenu. What does the guide say?",
     ["Pick one; put an “Overview” link in the menu", "Keep both but enlarge the chevron", "Make the link work on click and the menu on hover", "Allow it only on desktop screens"],
     "A row navigates or opens a menu, never both.")),
  ("Hover is decoration",
   "Activation is **click, tap, Enter or Space** — never hover.\n\nHover can't open, close or dismiss anything, change ARIA state, or affect tab order. Moving the pointer away does **not** close an open menu.\n\nDismissal: re-activate the trigger, click outside, or press Esc — with focus returning to the trigger.", None,
   q("A user opens a menu, then moves the mouse away. What should happen?",
     ["The menu stays open", "The menu closes after a short delay", "The menu closes immediately", "The first item receives focus"],
     "Hover is visual only; it can't dismiss anything.")),
  ("Semantics and targets",
   "Use **native HTML before ARIA**: a landmark for the nav, real buttons and links, and `aria-expanded` on menu triggers.\n\nDon't put `role=\"menu\"` on site navigation — that role is for application menus, and it changes the keyboard model users expect.\n\nTargets are at least **24×24 px** and spaced apart, and focus is visible and clearly distinct from hover.", None,
   q("Which attribute belongs on a navigation menu trigger?",
     ["aria-expanded", "role=\"menu\"", "aria-live", "tabindex=\"-1\""],
     "It communicates the expanded or collapsed state; role=\"menu\" is for application menus."))],
 [q("Which inputs may activate a navigation item?",
    ["Click, tap, Enter or Space", "Click, tap or hover", "Any pointer or keyboard event", "Click and long-press only"],
    "Hover is explicitly excluded."),
  q("What is the minimum target size in the guide?",
    ["24×24 px", "16×16 px", "32×32 px", "44×44 px"],
    "And targets must be spaced apart."),
  q("Why avoid role=\"menu\" on site navigation?",
    ["It sets up an application keyboard model users don't expect", "Screen readers ignore it entirely", "It conflicts with landmark roles", "It is deprecated in current ARIA versions"],
    "Native HTML semantics come first."),
  q("What happens to focus when a menu is dismissed with Esc?",
    ["It returns to the trigger", "It moves to the first page heading", "It stays where it was", "It moves to the next navigation item"],
    "Returning focus keeps keyboard users oriented."),
  q("Why did Calabro publish this as a spec?",
    ["So it can be handed straight to engineering", "To replace the W3C navigation guidance", "Because his earlier articles were too theoretical", "To standardise navigation across design systems"],
    "It is the document he had refined over ten years of writing similar ones.")],
 summary="Click, tap, Enter or Space — never hover. A row is a link or a menu button, never both.")

write("onboarding-help",
 "Help works when it appears at the moment of the decision, inside the workflow, written from the user's task. Tours, front-loaded wizards and tooltips carrying essential instructions are the patterns decades of data say don't work.",
 [("Just in time, not up front",
   "**Just-in-time** — information appears when the user makes the next decision, not before they need to remember it.\n\n**Only what's necessary** — up-front steps only for what the system needs to work, or for security and compliance.\n\n**Explicit next action** — the minimum text needed to make the next step obvious. That is not the same as minimum text.",
   cmp("Easy to ship, rarely works", ["Product tour before any task", "Optional setup forced up front", "A ? icon holding key information", "Floating help bubble, bottom right"],
       "Just in time and in flow", ["Guidance at first meaningful use", "Ask only what the system needs now", "Required help visible on the screen", "Labels, helper text, empty states"]),
   q("What is the difference between “explicit next action” and “minimum text”?",
     ["Enough words to make the step obvious", "Fewer words, in a larger type size", "A single sentence per screen, always", "Text replaced by illustrations where possible"],
     "Brevity is not the goal; clarity about the next step is.")),
  ("Relevance and recovery",
   "**Relevant only** — adapt to role, permissions, product access and workflow stage. Help for features a user can't reach is noise.\n\n**Prevent and recover** — structure, validation and review first; then clear correction when errors do happen.\n\n**Help inside the flow** — don't depend on separate docs the user has to go and find.", None,
   q("An admin-only feature is explained in the tour every user sees. Which principle does this break?",
     ["Relevant only", "Just-in-time", "Prevent and recover", "Explicit next action"],
     "Help should adapt to role, permissions and workflow stage.")),
  ("Why this keeps happening",
   "Tools like Pendo make tours and tooltips **easy to add** — which is exactly why they get added.\n\nCalabro wrote the framework as a cross-team standard so onboarding stops becoming “a maze of popups, tours and misunderstood help widgets”.\n\nThe principles for any analytics or guidance tool: set the strategy before tagging anything, and start from the top tasks.", None,
   q("Why does Calabro single out tools like Pendo?",
     ["They make the weak patterns trivial to add", "Their analytics are inaccurate", "They can't be made accessible", "They conflict with most design systems"],
     "Ease of shipping is how the maze of popups accumulates."))],
 [q("When should help appear?",
    ["At the moment of the decision", "Immediately after signup", "At the end of the first session", "Whenever the user has been idle"],
    "Just-in-time, inside the workflow."),
  q("Which up-front step is justified?",
    ["Something the system needs to function", "A guided tour of the main features", "A preferences questionnaire", "An invitation to connect integrations"],
    "Or security and compliance requirements."),
  q("What's wrong with putting essential instructions in a tooltip?",
    ["Required help has to be visible on the screen", "Tooltips are hard to translate", "Tooltips slow the page down", "Tooltips can't contain formatted text"],
    "An icon-only trigger hides what people need."),
  q("Where should required guidance live instead of a separate help site?",
    ["Inside the flow", "In a searchable knowledge base", "In the welcome email", "In a downloadable PDF guide"],
    "Help inside the flow is one of the six principles."),
  q("What does “prevent and recover” put first?",
    ["Structure, validation and review", "A clear error message", "An undo button on every action", "A support contact link"],
    "Prevention first; clear correction when errors still happen.")],
 summary="Help at the moment of the decision, inside the flow — not tours, wizards and tooltips.")

write("interaction-cost",
 "Interaction cost is the total effort — physical and mental — people spend to get something done. In daily-use B2B tools those costs repeat hundreds of times.",
 [("Four places cost hides",
   "**Physical** — clicks, scrolls, typing, pointing.\n**Mental** — reading, deciding, remembering.\n**Recovery** — errors, undo, backtracking.\n**Repetition** — multiply all of the above by how often the task is done.\n\nA small cost in a task done once is noise. The same cost in a task done 40 times a day is a productivity problem.", None,
   q("Which multiplier turns a small friction into a real problem?",
     ["How often the task is performed", "How many users have access", "How long the product has existed", "How complex the underlying system is"],
     "Repetition is the fourth component of the cost.")),
  ("Unnecessary interactions are the worst kind",
   "Calabro's definition: the total effort people spend using anything they interface with.\n\n**Unnecessary** interactions stand out, because users are forced to repeat them every time — and that's what turns tolerant users into jaded, angry ones.",
   cmp("Costs users accept", ["Steps the task genuinely needs", "A confirmation before deletion", "Reading a result they asked for"],
       "Costs that breed resentment", ["Re-entering data the system has", "A dialog that adds nothing", "Navigating back to where you were"]),
   q("Why are unnecessary interactions worse than necessary ones of the same size?",
     ["Users repeat them every single time", "They are harder to measure", "They usually break accessibility", "They cost more to implement"],
     "Repeated pointless effort is what makes users resentful.")),
  ("How to find it",
   "**Task analysis and timing** — GOMS-KLM, PURE ratings, benchmark tests and cognitive walkthroughs show where steps and time accumulate.\n\n**Observation in context** — contextual inquiry, shadowing and longitudinal studies reveal repetition, interruptions and workarounds that labs miss.\n\n**System data** — drop-off rates, error logs, undo frequency and repeat attempts measure cost objectively.\n\nUse it early: treat interaction cost as a design input, not a post-launch metric.", None,
   q("Which signal in your system data points at recovery cost?",
     ["Undo frequency", "Session length", "Daily active users", "Feature adoption rate"],
     "Undo, error logs and repeat attempts measure the cost of recovering from mistakes."))],
 [q("What does interaction cost measure?",
    ["Total physical and mental effort to get something done", "Time on task for a single workflow", "The development cost of an interaction", "The number of clicks to reach a page"],
    "Clicks are only the physical part."),
  q("Which method reveals repetition that a lab study misses?",
    ["Shadowing in context", "A moderated usability test", "A preference survey", "A heuristic evaluation"],
    "Contextual inquiry and longitudinal studies show real working conditions."),
  q("When should interaction cost be considered?",
    ["Early, as a design input", "After launch, as a metric", "During QA, alongside bug triage", "Only when users complain"],
    "Treat it as an input, not a post-launch measurement."),
  q("Which of these is a mental cost?",
    ["Deciding which option applies", "Scrolling a long page", "Typing an address", "Clicking a submit button"],
    "Reading, deciding and remembering are the mental component."),
  q("Why does Calabro call this an old concept worth bringing back?",
    ["It applies to every review, and teams have stopped using it", "It was disproved and later re-validated", "It predates usability testing as a method", "Modern analytics tools can finally measure it"],
    "He wants it back in every design review.")],
 summary="Physical + mental + recovery effort, multiplied by how often the task repeats.")

write("cognitive-load",
 "“Reduce cognitive load” is too blunt. Sweller's theory splits it into three kinds, and each one calls for a different response.",
 [("Three kinds of load",
   "**Intrinsic** — the task's own complexity. Calculating returns is harder than reading a price. You can't remove it; you can chunk it.\n\n**Extraneous** — effort caused by the design: clutter, poor layout, jargon. **Remove this.**\n\n**Germane** — effort that builds a useful mental model. Support it with consistent patterns.",
   cmp("Can't remove", ["Intrinsic: the task is genuinely hard", "Chunk it across steps or screens"],
       "Remove or support", ["Extraneous: clutter, jargon, layout", "Germane: effort that builds understanding"]),
   q("A tax form is hard because tax is hard. Which load is that?",
     ["Intrinsic", "Extraneous", "Germane", "Recovery"],
     "You can chunk it into steps, but you can't design it away.")),
  ("The hospital example",
   "On a hospital medication system, nurses faced medication lists, patient details and schedules crammed onto one screen.\n\nFieldwork showed the **intrinsic** load was unavoidable. The team **chunked** information across screens to cut the extraneous load, and simplified the language to support learning.\n\nErrors dropped and efficiency rose. Nurses disliked the change at first; within a few months they were fully on board.", None,
   q("What does the hospital example suggest about user reaction to load reduction?",
     ["Initial dislike doesn't mean the change is wrong", "Users always prefer fewer items per screen", "Clinical users resist all interface change", "Training matters more than layout"],
     "Errors dropped and efficiency rose; acceptance followed within months.")),
  ("Measuring it: NASA-TLX",
   "When you need numbers, **NASA-TLX** measures perceived workload on six dimensions: mental demand, physical demand, temporal demand, performance, effort and frustration.\n\nIn practice: chunk complex tasks into steps, strip elements that don't serve the primary task, and use consistent patterns so effort goes into learning the domain rather than the UI.", None,
   q("What does NASA-TLX measure?",
     ["Perceived workload on six dimensions", "Task completion time under pressure", "Error rates in safety-critical systems", "The ratio of intrinsic to extraneous load"],
     "It is a subjective workload instrument, not an objective timing method."))],
 [q("Where does cognitive load theory come from?",
    ["Educational psychology, via John Sweller", "Human factors research in aviation", "Nielsen Norman Group's heuristics", "Cognitive neuroscience in the 2000s"],
    "Late 1980s, before UX adopted it."),
  q("Which load should you remove outright?",
    ["Extraneous", "Intrinsic", "Germane", "All three, as far as possible"],
    "It is effort the design added, serving nothing."),
  q("What do you do with intrinsic load?",
    ["Chunk it", "Remove it", "Measure it with NASA-TLX", "Shift it to a help article"],
    "The complexity belongs to the task; break it into steps."),
  q("What is germane load?",
    ["Effort that builds a useful mental model", "Load caused by inconsistent patterns", "Load that varies by user expertise", "The residual load after a redesign"],
    "Support it with consistency so effort goes into the domain, not the UI."),
  q("Why is “reduce cognitive load” too blunt as advice?",
    ["Only one of the three kinds should be removed", "Load can't be measured reliably", "It ignores physical effort entirely", "Users differ too much for a general rule"],
    "Cut the extraneous, respect the intrinsic, support the germane.")],
 summary="Intrinsic, extraneous, germane — cut one, chunk one, support one. NASA-TLX when you need numbers.")
print("interaction module written")
